1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:04,890
This is our Community Safety Resource Planner.

2
00:00:04,910 --> 00:00:10,250
Our user is a program manager at the LA Bureau of Street Lighting,

3
00:00:10,270 --> 00:00:12,670
deciding which LAPD reporting districts

4
00:00:12,690 --> 00:00:16,810
get an on-site lighting assessment first.

5
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:19,510
The app sets assessment priority only.

6
00:00:19,530 --> 00:00:20,750
It doesn't predict anything,

7
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:25,150
and it doesn't show that lighting causes offenses.

8
00:00:26,890 --> 00:00:33,630
The overview counts nighttime reported offenses, 6 p.m. to 6 a.m.,

9
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:38,250
from January 2025 to September 2026.

10
00:00:38,270 --> 00:00:46,430
That's about 179,000 offenses in 1,164 districts.

11
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:50,010
Persistence is 90%:

12
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:56,850
a typical district has nighttime reports in nine of every ten months.

13
00:00:56,870 --> 00:01:00,250
And almost every offense can be mapped.

14
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:07,640
The ranked table shows which districts to assess first.

15
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:14,520
The score, from 0 to 100, is 60% frequency, how many reports,

16
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:19,020
and 40% persistence, how many months they appear in,

17
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:22,000
so one short spike can't reach the top.

18
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:29,800
District 0182 in Central ranks first, at 87.68.

19
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:35,860
The map shows where these reports are; darker means more.

20
00:01:37,710 --> 00:01:41,190
The filters update everything together.

21
00:01:41,210 --> 00:01:42,970
With only Central selected,

22
00:01:42,990 --> 00:01:48,090
we get 56 districts and about 14,000 offenses.

23
00:01:48,710 --> 00:01:54,450
0182 is still first, but its score drops to 86.07,

24
00:01:54,470 --> 00:01:58,590
because it's now compared with Central districts only,

25
00:01:58,610 --> 00:02:00,050
not the whole city.

26
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:01,970
Now I'll reset.

27
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:08,860
Clicking a row opens District Evidence.

28
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:13,580
Is the pattern steady, or concentrated in a few months?

29
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:19,480
For 0182, it's steady, around 40 to 80 a month.

30
00:02:20,140 --> 00:02:26,100
The last month looks low only because the data ends in mid-September.

31
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:30,800
The top offense is burglary from vehicles,

32
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:34,360
and the top premise is parking lots,

33
00:02:34,380 --> 00:02:37,300
so the crew knows what to inspect.

34
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:43,120
Compare puts districts side by side.

35
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:55,960
The top two, 0182 and 1494, are almost tied: 87.68 versus 87.63.

36
00:02:56,740 --> 00:03:03,020
0182 has more reports overall and more in the last three months,

37
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:05,140
and both are steady.

38
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:09,480
The app shows the evidence; the manager decides.

39
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:15,140
Now two edge cases.

40
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:20,760
If I select no areas, the app shows zero and says nothing matches,

41
00:03:20,780 --> 00:03:23,700
instead of quietly showing all the data.

42
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:30,120
District 06 has 25 reports but no valid coordinates.

43
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:31,400
They're still counted,

44
00:03:31,420 --> 00:03:36,660
but the map shows a message instead of points in the wrong place.

45
00:03:37,820 --> 00:03:44,480
The Worklist CSV exports this list for the field team.

46
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:49,640
Our biggest limitation: there's no streetlight data yet,

47
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:54,740
so we rank by reported offenses, not lighting conditions.

48
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:58,180
Next, we'd add the city's streetlight map,

49
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:03,480
so the manager can see nearby lights before sending a crew.

50
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:04,420
Thank you.

